Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> On 27.05.2012 02:31, Rob Cypher wrote:
>> What I Like:
>> 
>> Color-based potion alchemy
>
> The possibility of mixing cheap potions to get powerful ones looks not
> too appealing, balance-wise, I think.

Well, that's already the case in vanilla Nethack.  I don't see
any of the potions as being so incredible powerful that we
should worry about the possibility that some characters could
potentially make a few more.

> With alchemy I think another Nethack issue should be adjusted; if you
> intend to mix large stacks of potions the probability of exploding in
> that process should increase (with something like a quadratic function).
> Maybe a fail rate of: f = 10*(stacksize/8)^2  (truncated to int) which
> results in success for 1-2 potions, up to 10% failure until 8 potions,
> 50% failure with 18 potions, 90% at 24 potions, and alchemizing stacks
> larger than 25 will always fail.

Seems to me that the real problem is that you can #dip 25
potions of A into 1 potion of B and make 25 potions of A+B.
There ought to be some sort of ratio required of A to B.
Maybe if you mix stacks of A and B with a ratio of 3/4 or
higher, then you end up with a full-strength potion; 1/2 to
3/4, then you end up with a diluted potion, and less than 1/2
won't work (maybe you end up with just a diluted potion of A).

Diluted potions could also be changed to be less effective
than the full-strength.

